  "context_pill": "PGBP - Section 43B Deductions",
  "example_summary": "Deduction of interest on loans converted into fresh loans under Section 43B.",
  "source_provision": "| Category | Provision | Statutory Condition |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Scope | Section 43B(d) / 43B(e) | Interest on loans from specified financial institutions or scheduled banks |\n| Timing | Actual Payment Rule | Deduction allowable only in the Previous Year in which payment is actually made |\n| Exclusion | Explanation 3C | Conversion of unpaid interest into a loan is NOT considered 'actual payment' |\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] Statutory Trigger:\n> The deduction for interest payable to financial institutions and banks is subject to the 'Actual Payment' condition under Section 43B. Even if the assessee follows the mercantile system of accounting, the tax benefit is deferred until the cash outflow occurs.\n\n> [!WARNING] The Conversion Trap:\n> When an assessee defaults on interest and enters into a restructuring agreement where the interest is converted into a fresh loan, Section 43B explicitly bars the deduction upon such conversion. The deduction is deferred until the subsequent repayment of the converted loan installments.",
